/-!
# An invariant affine generic neighbourhood with a flat thickening

Source: Stacks Project, More on Groupoids, Tag 0ABC,
`lemma-invariant-affine-open-around-generic-point`
(blueprint: lem-sfb-finite-groupoid-generic-affine-flat-thickening).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Finitely many generic orbits have a common invariant affine neighbourhood with an
invariant affine thickening of finite presentation and finite locally free restricted arrows.
The ideal `J` represents the closed subscheme `V'` of `V`. Its two pullbacks are compared
on the common open arrow scheme using the canonical restriction of the target. The last
clauses use the actual double restriction `V' ×[U,s] R ×[t,U] V'` and its two projections.
Source: Stacks Project, More on Groupoids, Tag 0ABC,
`lemma-invariant-affine-open-around-generic-point`
(blueprint: lem-sfb-finite-groupoid-generic-affine-flat-thickening).

Proof plan (the nine steps of the node):
1. If `m = 0`, take both schemes empty. Otherwise the invariant rank filtration
   of `lem-sfb-finite-presented-groupoid-rank-filtration` partitions the finite union
   `F` of the selected orbits into finitely many rank strata. Finiteness of the orbits
   follows from `lem-weil-pbc-finite-closed-map-finite-fibres`.
2. Each selected generic point in a stratum lies in its interior by
   `lem-sfb-generic-point-fp-closed-interior`. For an open `E`, put
   `H(E) = U \\ t(s⁻¹(U \\ E))`. Closedness of the finite target makes this open;
   `lem-sfb-groupoid-subset-saturation-invariant` and inversion make it invariant,
   contained in `E`, and containing exactly the points whose entire orbits lie in `E`.
   Apply this to the stratum interiors to obtain disjoint invariant opens `W_r`.
3. On their union `W`, restrict `Z_(r-1)` to `W_r`. The resulting closed subschemes
   glue on these disjoint pieces to an invariant thickening `W'` containing `F`.
   Its ideal is locally finitely generated by
   `lem-trf-closed-immersion-finite-presentation-ideal`, hence it is finitely presented.
4. On each piece the restricted source is the rank-filtration source, finite locally
   free of that rank. Invariance identifies the double restriction with the single
   pullback; `lem-weil-pbc-finite-morphism-permanence` supplies finiteness. Flatness
   and local finite presentation are local on these pieces. Inversion gives the target.
   Pullbacks of the nil ideal are nil, identifying the old and new underlying orbits.
5. Closed affine pullbacks of affine opens in `W` cover `X = W'`; their intersections
   are quasi-compact by `pqf-lem-qs-intersection-qc` and closed pullback. The criterion
   `lem-weil-pbc-quasi-separated-affine-cover-criterion` makes `X` quasi-separated.
   The homeomorphism preserves the generic points, so
   `lemma-nagata-affine-neighbourhood-generic-points-exists` supplies an affine open
   `A` containing the whole finite set `F`.
6. The invariant open `B = H(A)` contains `F`. The basic-open basis from
   `lem-weil-pbc-spec-localization-away-basic-open` and
   `lem-weil-pbc-prime-avoidance` give a section `f` with `F ⊆ D(f) ⊆ B`.
7. Set `V' = H(D(f))`. Inversion identifies its set with the norm locus of
   `lem-pd-descent-norm-locus-affine`; apply that lemma to deduce affineness.
8. Lift this open through `|W'| ≃ |W|` to `V`. Restricting the ideal gives a finitely
   presented thickening `V' → V`. By `lem-sc-closed-affine-union-affine`, `V` is affine.
   The pullback homeomorphism from step 4 gives open invariance of `V`; restricting
   the equal pulled-back ideals of `W'` gives scheme-theoretic invariance of `V'`.
9. The selected points lie in `V'`. Its arrows are the displayed double fibre product.
   The restricted source is an open restriction of the finite locally free source
   in step 4, hence finite locally free; inversion gives the same for the target.
-/
theorem groupoid_exists_generic_affine_flat_thickening
    {U R : Scheme.{u}} (s t : R ⟶ U) (e : U ⟶ R) (i : R ⟶ R)
    (c : pullback s t ⟶ R) (hG : IsGroupoidScheme s t e i c)
    [IsFinite s] [IsFinite t]
    [LocallyOfFinitePresentation s] [LocallyOfFinitePresentation t]
    [QuasiSeparatedSpace U] (m : ℕ) (p : Fin m → U)
    (hgeneric : ∀ (j : Fin m) (x : U), x ∈ t '' (s ⁻¹' {p j}) →
      ∃ Z ∈ irreducibleComponents U, IsGenericPoint x Z) :
    ∃ (V : U.Opens) (hV : s ⁻¹ᵁ V = t ⁻¹ᵁ V) (J : V.toScheme.IdealSheafData),
      IsAffine V.toScheme ∧ IsAffine J.subscheme ∧
      (∀ j : Fin m, p j ∈ Set.range (J.subschemeι ≫ V.ι)) ∧
      LocallyOfFinitePresentation J.subschemeι ∧
      QuasiCompact J.subschemeι ∧ QuasiSeparated J.subschemeι ∧
      IsHomeomorph J.subschemeι ∧
      J.comap (s ∣_ V) = J.comap (t.resLE V (s ⁻¹ᵁ V) (le_of_eq hV)) ∧
      (pullback.fst (pullback.snd (J.subschemeι ≫ V.ι) s ≫ t)
          (J.subschemeι ≫ V.ι) ≫
        pullback.fst (J.subschemeι ≫ V.ι) s).IsFiniteLocallyFree ∧
      (pullback.snd (pullback.snd (J.subschemeι ≫ V.ι) s ≫ t)
        (J.subschemeι ≫ V.ι)).IsFiniteLocallyFree := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
